\end{equation*}
where the first two inequalities follow from the fact that for random variables $(X, Y, S, T)$ it is always the case that $\bar{H}( X | S, T) \leq \bar{H}(X, Y | S, T) \leq \bar{H}(X, Y | S)$ (the second inequality also uses the fact that $(\bvec{U}M)_{<j}$ is a sub-matrix of $\bvec{U}M_0$). The equality follows from the definition of $\tilde{\bvec{U}}_i$ and the fact that $f(\cdot)$ is  deterministic function. The final inequality follows from the fact that conditioning can only decrease the entropy.

Given $\tilde{\bvec{U}} M_0$ we can produce estimate $\hat{\bvec{U}} := \argmin_{\bvec{V}} \{ \mathop\mathrm{wt}(\bvec{V}) : \bvec{V} M_0 = \tilde{\bvec{U}} M_0\}$, where $\mathop\mathrm{wt}(\bvec{V}) = |\{j : \bvec{V}_j \not=0 \}|$.

We note that if $\mathop\mathrm{wt}(\tilde{\bvec{U}}) \leq b$ then $\hat{\bvec{U}} = \tilde{\bvec{U}}$. Indeed, we have $\mathop\mathrm{wt}(\hat{\bvec{U}}) \leq \mathop\mathrm{wt}(\tilde{\bvec{U}})$, therefore $\mathop\mathrm{wt}(\hat{\bvec{U}} - \tilde{\bvec{U}}) \leq 2 \mathop\mathrm{wt}(\tilde{\bvec{U}}) \leq 2 b$, but on the other hand $(\hat{\bvec{U}} - \tilde{\bvec{U}}) M_0 = 0$, and by the assumption on distance of $\ker M_0^T$ we deduce that $\hat{\bvec{U}} - \tilde{\bvec{U}} = 0$. Therefore $\P(\tilde{\bvec{U}} \not= \hat{\bvec{U}}) \leq \P(\mathop\mathrm{wt}(\tilde{\bvec{U}}) > b)$. All coordinates of $\tilde{\bvec{U}}$ are independent, and each $\tilde{\bvec{U}}_i$ is nonzero with probability at most $\delta$, therefore
\begin{equation*}
